For the benefit of those who are uninitiated with anonymous FTP:
 
To obtain the source for NetHack, you must first open a connection to
 "linc.cis.upenn.edu".

This is usually (?) accomplished by typing
"ftp linc.cis.upenn.edu", or, if that returns an error to the effect of
 _unknown_host_, then try
"ftp 130.91.6.8"
If neither of these work, ask your local system adminstrator how to FTP.
You may not be able to from your site.

Once the connection has been established, the remote system will prompt you
for a username.  DO NOT USE YOUR OWN NAME!  (unless you happen to have an
account on linc.cis.upenn.edu.  I doubt it.)

At the username prompt, enter "anonymous".
The system will now (usually) say "guest login ok.", and proceed to ask you
for a password.  For the password, enter your mailing address.  This is
almost always your username followed by '@' followed by your mailserver.
For exmample, *I* would enter as password  <myname>@ccu.umanitoba.ca   ...like 
so.  If you're not 100% sure of your mailing address, ask someone.  It's not
ABSOLUTELY critical, but it's a nice courtesy to the System Administrator.

Now, you should be sitting at a prompt that looks like  "ftp>".
Enter "cd pub/NH3.0".
You are now in the NetHack sources directory.  type
"get README Files READ_FIRST Porting"
if you are going to compile on an IBM PC, then add "PC" to the end of that list.

Now, go for your lunch break.  When you come back, you may have to take your
coffee break, too.
[Note:  For those of you familiar with job control, you can suspend this
 process and push it into the background!  ..just expect some output every now
 and then! ]

When the transfers have finished, type "close" at the "ftp>" prompt.
Next, type "quit".  You are now out of FTP.  

Now, take a CLOSE look at the files you transfered.  They are all standard
UNIX text format, suitable for viewing on your screen.  After you have looked at
these, if you STILL want to get the sources, logon to linc.cis.upenn.edu as
described here, but instead of typing "get README Files......", type
"get <filename>" for EVERY file listed in the file 'Files'.  The files you will
have already transfered the first time explain how to compile.

